WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.350
Keeping yourself alive is hard work.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.210
There are bacteria covered children ready
to give you numerous diseases.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:13.460
There’s delicious junk food that will swell
your heart up and make it stop working.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.000
And there are evil monsters hiding under your
bed, waiting to eat your face, sorry kids

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.010
but it’s true.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.539
Most of the time though, we rely on our doctors
to make sure we don’t accidentally trip

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:26.060
and fall face first into an early grave.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.490
They help us to make the decisions about how
best to keep all the nasty stuff at bay, not

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.469
that we always take their advice that is;
because you know, there’s stuff like doughnuts

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:36.270
and cheesecake in the world.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.170
But are there situations where a doctor can
say “actually, you know what, I’m afraid

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.870
your time is up”?

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.690
When times are tough or everything has just
gone wrong, how much say does a doctor have

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.159
in whether you should continue to live or
not.

00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:59.199
Can a doctor legally kill their patients?

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:03.129
In most parts of the modern world, if you
rushed in to an emergency room with blood

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:07.500
spraying all over the place or a limb dangling
off, you’d expect the nearest doctor to

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.880
tell Mrs Johnson her bowel movement diary
will have to wait ‘til next Monday and run

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.310
over to help you.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:18.690
But throughout history, especially while at
war, doctors couldn’t possibly attend all

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.960
the patients they had so they would have to
choose who to help.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.570
For a long time, this would have been done
at random, doctors picking the people nearest

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.159
to them or simply the ones who smelt the most
pleasant.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:35.960
But as medicine became increasingly scientific
and statistical, doctors realized that who

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.820
they helped, and in what order, could have
a big impact on the long term survival rates

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.110
of troops.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:47.499
During the Napoleonic wars and World War I,
French doctors began using a system called

00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:53.179
triage, where they would arrange the injured
into three sections; those likely to live

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:58.890
even WITHOUT treatment, those likely to die
even WITH treatment, and those for whom immediate

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.119
care might make a big difference in their
chances.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:05.859
So, if you’d been shot in the arm, you’d
have to sit and wait.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:10.621
If you’d been shot in the chest, please
just die quietly in the corner but you with

00:02:10.621 --> 00:02:15.400
the bleeding kidney, get over here so we can
sew you up and shut up all your noisy complaining.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.570
This basic model is still what a paramedic
might do today if they were dealing with 50

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.010
people on their own.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:28.540
But there is a surprising lack of consensus
on what to do in a major emergency and often,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.960
even hospitals do not have a concrete policy
for triage.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.140
In the aftermath of Hurricane Katrina, many
hospitals were cut off, without power and

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.850
had to make very difficult choices as the
flood water and temperature rose and their

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.950
supplies dwindled.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:49.890
In Memorial Medical Center, New Orleans, a
doctors and two nurses made the difficult

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:57.140
choice to give lethal drug doses to some patients
who were in a great deal of pain and had virtually

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.390
no chance of survival.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.440
The case against them for second degree murder
was eventually thrown out by the Louisiana

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.690
courts.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:13.300
So to answer the question, yes in this instance
a doctor was able to legally kill patients.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:19.870
But its wider impact was a realization that
doctors needed more of a framework to work

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.770
with in these horrible situations.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:27.950
Dr Daugherty Biddison, decided that these
life and death questions should have the input

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:33.570
of the general public and led a series of
forums to see what everyday people thought.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:38.200
There was general agreement that you should
prioritize the people with the best chance

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.960
of living longer but once that was set; how
do you chose within those groups of equal

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.680
chance survivors?

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.110
Do you pick parents over single people?

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.920
What happens to illegal immigrants?

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.440
Can you discriminate by age?

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.210
And if they are a Belieber, does that put
them at the bottom of every list?

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:01.550
These emergency extremes are fortunately quite
rare and hospitals have often dealt incredibly

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.930
well with a sudden influx of patients.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.370
The general public have reacted amazingly
well too; in the aftermath of the Boston marathon

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:15.910
bombings, some competitors who had just run
A MARATHON, kept running to the hospital in

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.380
order to give blood.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.310
Bet you feel pretty lazy now, huh?

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:26.150
But these life and death choices come up every
day in hospitals since we now have an amazing

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.090
ability to keep people alive when their body
is really letting the side down.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:36.530
Whatever organ malfunctions, there are machines
to do its work; from kidney dialysis to artificial

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.880
hearts and iron lungs.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.510
Even when someone is completely brain dead,
meaning they have no electrical activity in

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:48.100
their brain, we can keep the body going for
about a week.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:54.680
In 2005, the UK introduced the Mental Capacity
Act, which laid out guidelines for who gets

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.169
to make decisions about a patient’s future.

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:03.530
It states that you have the capacity to make
your own medical choices, even if they mean

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.010
certain death.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:11.760
It backs this up by saying that you can’t
be deemed incapable of making decisions just

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.280
because you make an unwise choice.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.070
It’s like how your friends still try to
listen to you even though you once said The

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.420
Wolverine was the best film ever made.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:26.040
But if you’re incapable then the choice
reverts to the doctor unless you have already

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.880
selected a person to speak for you such as
a parent, a friend or if you have no friends

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.850
maybe nominate a fortune cookie you found
on the shelf.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:41.780
So, you are allowed to refuse care, even if
it’s necessary to keep you alive, but what

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.510
about the opposite?

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.840
What about when you’re in a hurry to check
out and you want the doctor to be a little

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.460
generous on the old morphine?

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.000
Euthanasia was looked down on for hundreds
of years, especially among the Judeo-Christian

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.100
religions who claimed it went against the
sanctity of human life, in the same way people

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.790
who commit suicide were not allowed to be
buried in official cemeteries.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:09.430
But in 1870, a British school teacher called
Samuel Williams gave a speech in Birmingham

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:13.230
about giving chloroform to terminally ill
patients.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.800
The transcript was published widely and it
sparked an important debate.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:24.370
In the US, the first attempt to legalize euthanasia
came in 1906 in Ohio, when a wealthy woman

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.210
called Anna Hall started a campaign after
watching her mother fight a long and hopeless

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.810
battle with liver cancer.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.620
Of course, this isn’t just a decision for
doctors; it’s something the military have

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.080
faced for a long time.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.710
A Coup de Grace (“Coo de Gra”) or mercy
killing has happened for centuries when soldiers

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.470
take pity on an enemy who is in great pain.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:51.190
SAS soldier, Sergeant Colin Maclachlan is
currently under investigation for a claim

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.550
that he did this in Iraq.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.770
Mercy killings violate the Geneva Convention,
which are the rules of combat.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:04.280
As for the medical side of things, right now,
euthanasia is legal in The Netherlands, Belgium,

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.000
Colombia and Luxembourg.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.090
There is also the case of assisted suicide,
where the patient themselves are the one to

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:14.150
administer the treatment, usually some kind
of drug.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.870
This is legal in four countries and some US
states.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:23.330
But your best bet to avoid all of this scary
life and death stuff is to just live a good

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:27.860
healthy life and the next time your doctor
advises you to stop drinking so much and do

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.620
more exercise you should probably listen.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:39.470
Because remember, if he’s smart enough he
can probably kill you and get away with it.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:59.479
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

